---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const lanes = [
  ['china-to-saudi-arabia', 'China → Saudi Arabia'],
  ['china-to-uae', 'China → UAE'],
  ['china-to-qatar', 'China → Qatar'],
  ['china-to-kuwait', 'China → Kuwait'],
  ['china-to-oman', 'China → Oman'],
  ['china-to-bahrain', 'China → Bahrain'],
];
const modes = [
  ['fcl', 'FCL', 'Full containers: what a rate includes, what it excludes, and when a 40ft beats two 20ft.'],
  ['lcl', 'LCL', 'Groupage: chargeable weight versus volume, co-loading risk, and the ports where it works.'],
  ['air-freight', 'Air freight', 'Chargeable weight, Gulf airport choices, battery rules and the lanes where air is rational.'],
  ['ddp', 'DDP', 'Delivered duty paid: what the seller carries, what the buyer loses control of, and where it fails.'],
  ['customs-clearance', 'Customs clearance', 'Documents, valuation, inspection and the variance that decides demurrage.'],
  ['certification', 'Certification', 'SABER and SASO for Saudi, ESMA for the UAE, and the sector schemes that gate release.'],
  ['cost-and-transit', 'Cost and transit', 'The full landed-cost stack and realistic transit ranges by mode and gateway.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="China to GCC freight guides"
  description="Guides to China-to-GCC freight: sea, air and land modes, FCL and LCL, DDP, customs clearance, SABER certification, cost and transit time."
>
  <h1>China to GCC freight guides</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The guides are organised by the decision a shipper has to make, not by keyword. Each one separates
    the part of the cost that is freight from the part that is statutory, and says which figures come
    from a published tariff and which are working ranges.
  </p>

  <h2>By market</h2>
  <p>
    Country guides combine the ports, the customs and VAT position, the certification regime and the
    route context for one destination. Start here if you know where the goods are going but not how
    the market works.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {lanes.map(([slug, label]) => (
      <li><a href={`/guides/${slug}/`}>{label}</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>By mode and by problem</h2>
  <ul>
    {modes.map(([slug, label, blurb]) => (
      <li><a href={`/guides/${slug}/`}>{label}</a> — {blurb}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>How to read a guide</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide opens with a direct answer to the question in its title, then shows the working: the
    documents involved, the charges, the places where practice diverges from the published rule, and
    the mistakes that recur. Where a claim depends on a regulator, the page links to the regulator
    rather than to a forwarder's summary of it. Where the position is editorial — whether DDP is worth
    the premium, which port suits a given cargo — it is written as a judgement with its exception
    stated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two companion pages explain the standard behind the guides: the
    <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> for how sources are chosen and checked, and the
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a> for what has changed since publication.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
